 I think the principle only governs the premodifier span itself and doesn't alter how heads get tagged as SpecificDisease or DiseaseClass.\n\nI'm drafting the final principle as a nested markdown list: the core IF/THEN rule states that a disease-denoting span functioning as a prenominal attributive modifier of a non-disease head noun (gene, protein, cohort, population) should be tagged Modifier rather than its usual disease label, regardless of what category the overall compound belongs to. I'm adding negative constraints—this doesn't apply when the disease term is the head of its phrase or object of a heading preposition (standard disease rules apply there), it shouldn't be applied to ambiguous coordinated disease mentions like \"breast or ovarian cancer\" given inconsistent gold examples, and it doesn't convert non-disease premodifiers like gene symbols or ethnicity terms into disease entities. I also want to stress that only the disease sub-span gets tagged Modifier, not the entire compound, and that this stays consistent with existing Modifier/SpecificDisease/DiseaseClass definitions without conflicting with CompositeMention rules.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Disease Premodifier Embedded in a Non-Disease Compound:** IF a disease-denoting span (a disease name or disease-class noun) functions as a prenominal attributive modifier restricting a non-disease head noun (e.g., a gene, protein, cohort, case, population, or risk noun), THEN tag that disease sub-span as **Modifier**, even when it sits inside a larger compound whose overall category is a gene name, a demographic or cohort label, or a phrase containing an ethnicity, numeric, or other non-disease qualifier. Assign the label from the sub-span's own syntactic role, not from the category of the enclosing compound, and do not extend the label to the whole compound.\n  - **Negative constraint (does NOT apply):**\n    - When the disease span is the head of its noun phrase and refers to the disease itse